{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nNeko is playing with his toys on the backyard of Aki's house. Aki decided to play a prank on him, by secretly putting catnip into Neko's toys. Unfortunately, he went overboard and put an entire bag of catnip into the toys...It took Neko an entire day to turn back to normal. Neko reported to Aki that he saw a lot of weird things, including a trie of all correct bracket sequences of length $$$2n$$$.The definition of correct bracket sequence is as follows:  The empty sequence is a correct bracket sequence,  If $$$s$$$ is a correct bracket sequence, then $$$(\\,s\\,)$$$ is a correct bracket sequence,  If $$$s$$$ and $$$t$$$ are a correct bracket sequence, then $$$st$$$ is also a correct bracket sequence. For example, the strings \"(())\", \"()()\" form a correct bracket sequence, while \")(\" and \"((\" not.Aki then came up with an interesting problem: What is the size of the maximum matching (the largest set of edges such that there are no two edges with a common vertex) in this trie? Since the answer can be quite large, print it modulo $$$10^9 + 7$$$.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class C_4_Variant1 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(System.out);\n        StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());\n        long a = Long.parseLong(st.nextToken());\n        long b = Long.parseLong(st.nextToken());\n        \n        List<Long> divisors = new ArrayList<>();\n        long diff = Math.abs(a - b);\n        long sqrtDiff = (long) Math.sqrt(diff);\n        \n        for (long i = 1; i <= sqrtDiff; i++) {\n            if (diff % i == 0) {\n                divisors.add(i);\n                divisors.add(diff / i);\n            }\n        }\n        \n        long minLcm = lcm(a, b);\n        long bestK = 0;\n        \n        for (long d : divisors) {\n            long p = d - Math.min(a, b) % d;\n            long newLcm = lcm(a + p, b + p);\n            if (newLcm < minLcm) {\n                minLcm = newLcm;\n                bestK = p;\n            } else if (newLcm == minLcm) {\n                bestK = Math.min(bestK, p);\n            }\n        }\n        \n        pw.println(bestK);\n        pw.close();\n    }\n    \n    static long gcd(long p, long q) {\n        while (q != 0) {\n            long temp = q;\n            q = p % q;\n            p = temp;\n        }\n        return p;\n    }\n    \n    static long lcm(long p, long q) {\n        return p / gcd(p, q) * q;\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class C_4_Variant1 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(System.out);\n        StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n        \n        int MOD = 1000000007;\n        \n        // The problem asks for the size of maximum matching in the trie of all correct bracket sequences of length 2n\n        // This is equivalent to counting the number of nodes in the trie that have at least one child, \n        // but the actual solution is known to be the Catalan number C(n) = (1/(n+1)) * C(2n, n)\n        // However, for maximum matching in a trie of bracket sequences, the answer is actually the nth Catalan number\n        \n        // Precompute factorials and inverse factorials for modular arithmetic\n        long[] fact = new long[2 * n + 1];\n        long[] invFact = new long[2 * n + 1];\n        \n        fact[0] = 1;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= 2 * n; i++) {\n            fact[i] = (fact[i - 1] * i) % MOD;\n        }\n        \n        // Compute modular inverse using Fermat's little theorem\n        invFact[2 * n] = modPow(fact[2 * n], MOD - 2, MOD);\n        for (int i = 2 * n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            invFact[i] = (invFact[i + 1] * (i + 1)) % MOD;\n        }\n        \n        // Catalan number C(n) = (1/(n+1)) * C(2n, n) = (2n)! / (n! * (n+1)!)\n        long catalan = (fact[2 * n] * invFact[n] % MOD) * invFact[n] % MOD;\n        catalan = catalan * modPow(n + 1, MOD - 2, MOD) % MOD;\n        \n        pw.println(catalan);\n        pw.close();\n    }\n    \n    static long modPow(long base, long exp, long mod) {\n        long result = 1;\n        base %= mod;\n        while (exp > 0) {\n            if ((exp & 1) == 1) {\n                result = (result * base) % mod;\n            }\n            base = (base * base) % mod;\n            exp >>= 1;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-2.9802276912960224e-06, -0.00012635385792236775, -2.62260095951205